/// Reads change data capture (CDC) events from DynamoDB Streams.
///
/// Consumes records from DynamoDB Streams with automatic checkpointing and shard
/// management.
///
/// DynamoDB Streams capture item-level changes in DynamoDB tables. This input supports:
///
/// - Automatic shard discovery and management
/// - Checkpoint-based resumption after restarts
/// - Concurrent processing of multiple shards
/// - Optional initial snapshot of existing table data
/// - Multi-table streaming with auto-discovery by tags or explicit table lists
///
/// ## Table discovery modes
///
/// This input supports three table discovery modes:
///
/// - `single` (default) - Stream from the tables specified in the `tables` field
/// - `tag` - Auto-discover and stream from multiple tables based on DynamoDB table tags.
///   Use `table_tag_filter` to filter tables (for example, `key:value`)
/// - `includelist` - Stream from an explicit list of tables specified in the `tables`
///   field. This mode is kept for backward compatibility. Use `single` instead.
///
/// When the input streams from more than one table, it reads from all matching tables
/// simultaneously. Each table maintains its own checkpoint state. In `tag` or
/// `includelist` mode, use `table_discovery_interval` to periodically rescan for new
/// tables (useful for dynamically tagged tables).
///
/// In `tag` mode, the input calls `dynamodb:ListTables`, then `dynamodb:DescribeTable`
/// and `dynamodb:ListTagsOfResource` on every table it lists. Grant those two actions on
/// every table in the region, not only on the tables that match the filter. A table the
/// input cannot describe or read tags from is skipped with a warning.
///
/// ## Prerequisites
///
/// The source DynamoDB table(s) must have [DynamoDB
/// Streams](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Streams.html)
/// enabled. You can enable streams with one of these view types:
///
/// - `KEYS_ONLY` - Only the key attributes of the modified item
/// - `NEW_IMAGE` - The entire item as it appears after the modification
/// - `OLD_IMAGE` - The entire item as it appeared before the modification
/// - `NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES` - Both the new and old item images
///
/// ## Snapshots
///
/// When `snapshot_mode` is set to `snapshot_only` or `snapshot_and_cdc`, or
/// `incremental_snapshot.enabled` is `true`, the input scans the entire table instead of,
/// before, or while streaming changes. This is useful for:
///
/// - Building a replica or cache with all existing data
/// - Syncing historical data to a data warehouse
/// - Populating a search index with existing records
///
/// The `snapshot_only` and `snapshot_and_cdc` modes are supported only when streaming
/// from a single table. With `table_discovery_mode: tag`, either mode fails at startup,
/// even when the filter matches only one table. An `includelist` with exactly one table
/// supports them, unless `signal_table_name` is set. A signal table makes the input
/// multi-table, so both modes then fail at startup. The incremental snapshot also works
/// in multi-table mode, including with `signal_table_name` set.
///
/// > **Warning:** Snapshots use the DynamoDB Scan API which consumes read capacity units
/// > (RCUs). For large tables, this can be expensive and take considerable time. Use
/// > `snapshot_segments` and `snapshot_throttle` to control RCU consumption.
///
/// > **Note:** In `snapshot_only` and `snapshot_and_cdc` modes, snapshots use eventually
/// > consistent reads and do not provide point-in-time consistency. Records modified
/// > during the snapshot may appear in both the snapshot and CDC stream (with different
/// > values). Use `snapshot_deduplicate` to minimize duplicates.
///
/// When `incremental_snapshot.enabled` is `true`, each table is backfilled page by page
/// while it streams, in single- or multi-table mode. In multi-table mode, this includes
/// tables found by periodic discovery. When `signal_table_name` is set, a table is
/// instead backfilled when a `snapshot-execute` signal names it, apart from the
/// exceptions listed under `signal_table_name`. A snapshot item is dropped when a stream
/// record for its key arrives while its page is held. So no snapshot item leaves the
/// input after a newer change to the same key. Once the stream catches up, the last event
/// the input emits for each key matches that key's current state in the table. This
/// ordering holds at the input. Output stages with `max_in_flight` above 1 can still
/// reorder messages. Incremental mode requires a stream view of `NEW_IMAGE` or
/// `NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES`, uses strongly consistent Scans (twice the RCU), and backfills
/// each table once unless a signal asks for it again. Each page waits a few seconds for
/// the stream to catch up before it is released, so for large tables raise
/// `snapshot_segments` and `snapshot_batch_size` (for example to 10 and 1000). A backfill
/// that fails is retried in the background from its acknowledged progress, while the
/// table keeps streaming. In multi-table mode, a backfill that fails to start is retried
/// the same way, and the other queued tables are backfilled meanwhile. In single-table
/// mode, a backfill that fails the connection is retried with its own backoff. Retries
/// wait 30 seconds at first and double up to 10 minutes between attempts. A failure that
/// only a permission or configuration change can fix (AWS returns
/// `AccessDeniedException`, `UnrecognizedClientException` or `ValidationException`, or
/// the checkpoint table is missing) waits 10 minutes from the first attempt. In
/// multi-table mode, a table deleted before its backfill completes is dropped from the
/// queue, and the input logs an error. The input does not retry it until it restarts.
///
/// ## Checkpointing
///
/// Checkpoints are stored in a separate DynamoDB table (configured with
/// `checkpoint_table`). This table is created automatically if it does not exist. On
/// restart, the input resumes from the last checkpointed position for each shard.
/// Snapshot progress is also checkpointed, allowing resumption mid-snapshot after
/// failures. The principal needs `dynamodb:UpdateItem` on the checkpoint table, in
/// addition to the read and write permissions it already uses. With
/// `incremental_snapshot.enabled: true`, or when `signal_table_name` is set, it also
/// needs `dynamodb:DeleteItem` on the checkpoint table, because resetting snapshot
/// progress and clearing a handled snapshot request both delete rows.
///
/// Without a namespace, checkpoints are keyed only by stream and shard (or by source
/// table and shard when `global_table` is enabled). Multiple pipelines that read the same
/// stream and share one `checkpoint_table` without a namespace overwrite each other's
/// positions, which causes skipped or duplicated events.
///
/// Multiple independent pipelines can share a single checkpoint table by giving each one
/// a distinct `checkpoint_namespace` (for example one namespace per developer or
/// environment). Namespaces isolate checkpoints from each other: a pipeline only sees
/// checkpoints written under its own namespace, so changing (or removing) the namespace
/// causes the pipeline to restart from `start_from`. Namespaces do not coordinate
/// consumers: two pipelines sharing the _same_ namespace still overwrite each other's
/// checkpoints.
///
/// The namespace is prefixed to the checkpoint key. When a pipeline restarts under a
/// namespace that has no checkpoints yet, it starts from `start_from`. With `start_from:
/// trim_horizon`, this replays all records still available in the stream's 24-hour
/// retention window. Switching back to a previously used namespace resumes from that
/// namespace's last checkpoints.
///
/// ### Ordering across shard splits
///
/// DynamoDB Streams splits shards over time. The input reads a child shard only after its
/// parent shard is read to the end, so the changes to an item keep their order. In these
/// cases the input reads a child shard without waiting, and an older change to an item
/// can arrive after a newer one:
///
/// - The first start with `start_from: latest`. Every shard starts at its latest
///   position.
/// - A parent shard that the input cannot open for several refresh cycles. A warning is
///   logged.
/// - A parent shard that the stream reports as not found.
///
/// ### Downtime longer than the stream retention
///
/// DynamoDB Streams keeps records for 24 hours. If the pipeline stops for longer, a
/// stored checkpoint can point at records that are gone, and the changes between the
/// checkpoint and the oldest kept record are lost. A warning is logged.
///
/// - With `snapshot_mode: none`, the shard resumes from `TRIM_HORIZON`.
/// - With `snapshot_mode: snapshot_and_cdc` and a completed snapshot, the input runs the
///   full snapshot again, so every item gets its current state. The CDC readers also
///   resume from `TRIM_HORIZON`, so expect duplicates. The intermediate changes are still
///   lost.
/// - With `incremental_snapshot.enabled: true` and a completed backfill, the input resets
///   the backfill and runs it again while the table streams. The CDC readers also resume
///   from `TRIM_HORIZON`. The intermediate changes are still lost.
///
/// ## Alternative
///
/// For better performance and longer retention (up to 1 year vs 24 hours), consider using
/// Kinesis Data Streams for DynamoDB with the `aws_kinesis` input instead.
///
/// ## Message format
///
/// Each change event is a JSON object with the fields `tableName`, `eventID`, `eventName`
/// (`INSERT`, `MODIFY`, or `REMOVE`), `eventVersion`, `eventSource`, and `awsRegion`,
/// plus a `dynamodb` object. The `dynamodb` object contains `keys`, `newImage`, and
/// `oldImage` when the stream view type provides them, along with `sequenceNumber`,
/// `streamViewType`, and `sizeBytes`. Snapshot records have an `eventName` of `READ`,
/// carry the whole item in `dynamodb.newImage`, and carry its primary key in
/// `dynamodb.keys`, as stream events do.
///
/// Attribute values are converted from DynamoDB's typed format, such as `{"S": "value"}`,
/// to plain values, such as `"value"`. Number attributes stay strings, so convert them in
/// a mapping if you need numeric values.
///
/// ## Metadata
///
/// This input adds the following metadata fields to each message:
